---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const beam = `import gleam/erlang/process
import gleam/json
import watershed
import watershed/channel.{type ChannelEvent}

type Msg {
  MapChanged(ChannelEvent)
}

pub fn main() {
  // Blocks until the op history has replayed locally.
  let assert Ok(doc) =
    watershed.connect(
      host: "127.0.0.1",
      port: 4000,
      tenant: "flow-co",
      document: "flowboard",
      token: token,
      user_id: "ada",
    )
  let board = watershed.root(doc)
  let events = watershed.subscribe(board)
  let selector =
    process.new_selector()
    |> process.select_map(events, MapChanged)

  // Subscribe first: this local write emits immediately.
  watershed.set(board, "title", json.string("Q3 sprint board"))

  // The same subject also receives remote changes as they are applied.
  let MapChanged(_event) = process.selector_receive_forever(selector)
}`;

const js = `import gleam/io
import gleam/json
import watershed_js.{WatershedConfig}

pub fn main() {
  let config =
    WatershedConfig(
      url: "ws://127.0.0.1:4000/socket/websocket?vsn=2.0.0",
      tenant: "flow-co",
      document: "flowboard",
      token: token,
      user_id: "ada",
    )

  // Returns immediately; on_ready fires after handshake + history replay.
  let doc =
    watershed_js.connect(config, on_ready: fn(result) {
      case result {
        Ok(Nil) -> io.println("synced")
        Error(reason) -> io.println("rejected: " <> reason)
      }
    })

  let board = watershed_js.root(doc)
}`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="connect"
  description="Step one of the watershed build guide: connect to a named, server-sequenced document, read its root map, and understand the optimistic-then-sequenced edit model, on both the BEAM and browser targets."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A document is a named container of channels</h2>
    <p>
      Everything watershed synchronises belongs to a <strong>document</strong>:
      a server-sequenced container addressed by a <code>tenant</code> and a
      <code>document</code> id. Every client that connects to the same pair joins
      the same live state. Flowboard’s teammates all open
      <code>flow-co / flowboard</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connecting replays the document’s op history so you start from the current
      converged state, then keeps the connection open to stream every subsequent
      change (yours and everyone else’s) in one server-assigned order.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Connect</h2>
    <p>
      watershed is BEAM-first, and the same pure core compiles to the browser.
      The facades share structure operations, but each target still constrains
      the connection and subscription APIs. Pick your target:
    </p>

    <div class="g-tabs">
      <input type="radio" name="target" id="t-beam" checked />
      <input type="radio" name="target" id="t-js" />
      <div class="g-tab-row" role="tablist" aria-label="Target">
        <label for="t-beam">BEAM · <code>watershed</code></label>
        <label for="t-js">Browser · <code>watershed_js</code></label>
      </div>
      <div class="g-tab-panels">
        <figure class="g-code g-panel g-panel-beam">
          <Code code={beam} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
          <figcaption class="annot">
            src/flowboard.gleam · erlang target
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="g-code g-panel g-panel-js">
          <Code code={js} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
          <figcaption class="annot">
            src/flowboard.gleam · javascript target
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The one real difference.</strong> On the BEAM,
        <code>connect</code> blocks and returns <code>Result(Document, _)</code>
        once history has replayed: natural inside an OTP process. In the
        browser it returns immediately and calls <code>on_ready</code> when the
        handshake completes, because you cannot block the event loop. Keep the
        handle when it arrives (it is fine for diagnostics and document-scoped
        services), but attach or ensure channels only after
        <code>on_ready</code> reports <code>Ok</code>. Treating handle-arrival
        and readiness as one event is a first-load race. Map
        subscriptions differ too: BEAM returns an OTP
        <code>Subject(MapEvent)</code>; the browser facade registers a callback
        directly. Step 05 hands the browser pattern to Lustre as effects.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Read the root, write optimistically</h2>
    <p>
      Every document has a root <code>SharedMap</code> at address
      <code>"root"</code>; get it with <code>root(doc)</code>. It reads like
      an ordinary map: <code>set</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>subscribe</code>.
      There is no merge callback to write and no conflict handler to forget.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>set</code> applies to your local replica <em>immediately</em> and
      emits a local subscription event, so subscribe before you make edits.
      Remote writes emit when the runtime applies them to your replica. Both
      event origins should re-read the visible state through the same render
      path. A server acknowledgement may emit nothing when it does not change
      that visible state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The server still assigns the order that resolves concurrent writes.
      Subscriptions tell your app when its local view changed; sequencing tells
      replicas which value wins. For a map, the last sequenced write wins per
      key.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storing a plain string under <code>"title"</code> works, but you don’t want
      raw key strings sprinkled through an app. Next we give the document a
      typed shape, so keys and their types are declared in one place.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open a live document from either target and read and write its root map,
      with local edits rendering instantly and converging in server order.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  /* target toggle — radios styled as printed cells, like the demo picker.
     Pure CSS: the checked radio reveals its panel and inks its label. */
  .g-tabs {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .g-tabs input {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .g-tab-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 100%;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .g-tab-row label {
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-annot);
    color: var(--muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
    transition:
      background var(--dur-feedback) var(--ease-out-quart),
      color var(--dur-feedback) var(--ease-out-quart);
  }

  .g-tab-row label:first-of-type {
    border-right: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .g-tab-row label code {
    font-size: 1em;
    color: inherit;
  }

  #t-beam:checked ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-beam"],
  #t-js:checked ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-js"] {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--bg);
  }

  #t-beam:focus-visible ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-beam"],
  #t-js:focus-visible ~ .g-tab-row label[for="t-js"] {
    outline: 2px solid var(--overprint);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .g-panel {
    display: none;
  }

  #t-beam:checked ~ .g-tab-panels .g-panel-beam,
  #t-js:checked ~ .g-tab-panels .g-panel-js {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
